---
import PageLayout from '../../layouts/PageLayout.astro';
import { LAST_UPDATED } from '../../data/site-config';
import { route } from '../../i18n/routes';
import { breadcrumbSchema, articleSchema } from '../../lib/seo';
import { displayDate } from '../../lib/format';
import { P, type SourceKey } from '../../lib/engine/params';
import { helpers } from '../../lib/helpers';
const lang = 'en';
const h = helpers(true);
const { E, m, m2, pc } = h;
const L = (k: SourceKey, t?: string) => `<a href="${P.sources[k].url}" target="_blank" rel="nofollow noopener">${t ?? P.sources[k].label.en}</a>`;
const q40 = E.qx(40, 'm');
const p25 = E.payoutChance(40, 25, 'm');
---
<PageLayout title="Cover Gap Method 2026/27: State Rates, ONS Tables and Limits" description="How Cover Gap works out cover needs, sick pay timelines and payout odds: DWP rates for 2026/27, ONS life tables 2022 to 2024, the tests and what is left out." lang={lang} jsonLd={[articleSchema({ title: 'How the figures are worked out', description: 'Method of Cover Gap', path: route('method', lang), lang }), breadcrumbSchema([{ name: 'Home', path: route('home', lang) }, { name: 'Method', path: route('method', lang) }])]} breadcrumbs={[{ label: 'Method' }]}>
  <header class="mb-6"><p class="kicker">Method</p><h1 class="mt-1 text-3xl font-bold text-navy-900 md:text-4xl">How the Cover Gap figures are worked out</h1></header>
  <div class="prose prose-slate max-w-none">
    <p>Every Cover Gap calculator calls one engine of pure functions, fed by two files: the parameter file of state rates and tax thresholds, and the ONS life tables. No amount is typed into a page. The parameter file was last checked against its sources on {displayDate(P.retrieved_at, 'en-GB')}; the life tables are the 2022-2024 edition, published by the ONS on {displayDate('2025-12-10', 'en-GB')}. Updates are made by hand when the DWP publishes new rates and when the ONS publishes a new edition; nothing is fetched automatically.</p>
    <h2>1. Life cover needed</h2>
    <p>Need = mortgage balance + other debts + funeral budget + yearly income needed × number of years + lump sums for children. From it come savings the family could use, death in service, personal cover already held and, where it is due, the full Bereavement Support Payment: for married couples and civil partners {m(P.bsp.higherLump)} plus {P.bsp.months} × {m(P.bsp.higherMonthly)} with a child for whom Child Benefit is paid, otherwise {m(P.bsp.standardLump)} plus {P.bsp.months} × {m(P.bsp.standardMonthly)}; for couples living together, the higher rate with such a child and nothing without (<Fragment set:html={L('gov_bsp')} />, <Fragment set:html={L('gov_rates')} />). The calculator assumes a claim within {P.bsp.fullClaimMonths} months and a qualifying National Insurance record. No interest or inflation is applied to the income.</p>
    <h2>2. Chance of a payout</h2>
    <p>For a person aged x, the chance of dying within a term of n years is 1 − (1 − q<sub>x</sub>)(1 − q<sub>x+1</sub>)…(1 − q<sub>x+n−1</sub>), where q is the ONS probability of dying before the next birthday, for the sex and nation chosen (<Fragment set:html={L('ons_nlt')} />, <Fragment set:html={L('ons_nlt_nations')} />). A fractional year uses the same rate for the fraction. Example: q<sub>40</sub> for men in the United Kingdom is {pc(q40, 3)}, and the chance of dying within 25 years from 40 is {pc(p25)}. Ages above 100 use the rate at 100. The tables are period tables: they apply today’s death rates to the future, with no allowance for improvement, so they overstate the risk slightly for long terms. They cover the whole population, not insured lives.</p>
    <h2>3. Over 50s and whole of life</h2>
    <p>The break-even age is the age at which the premium typed in, times the number of months, equals the sum assured; if premiums stop at a set age before that, there is no break-even. The chance of reaching that age uses the same tables. Life expectancy is the ONS e<sub>x</sub>. No premium is ever suggested.</p>
    <h2>4. Sick pay and New Style ESA</h2>
    <p>Weekly pay = monthly pay × 12 ÷ 52. Statutory Sick Pay from {h.D.ssp} is the lower of {m2(P.ssp.weekly)} and {pc(P.ssp.earningsShare)} of average weekly earnings, from the first day, for up to {P.ssp.maxWeeks} weeks (<Fragment set:html={L('gov_ssp')} />, <Fragment set:html={L('gov_ssp_2026')} />). Company sick pay is never below SSP in the weeks SSP is due. When company and statutory pay end, New Style ESA follows at the assessment rate ({m2(P.esa.assessmentUnder25)} under 25, {m2(P.esa.assessment25)} from 25) for {P.esa.assessmentWeeks} weeks, then {m2(P.esa.wrag)} in the work-related activity group, which stops after {P.esa.wragDays} days, or {m2(P.esa.support)} in the support group, with no end date (<Fragment set:html={L('gov_esa')} />). The engine assumes the National Insurance conditions are met and that ESA starts the week company pay ends. Self-employed people get no SSP and go straight to ESA. Income protection is added from the end of the deferred period. Amounts are before tax.</p>
    <h2>5. Inheritance tax on a payout</h2>
    <p>Payout to the estate: inheritance tax at {pc(P.iht.rate)} on the estate above the nil-rate band of {m(P.iht.nrb)}, plus the residence band of up to {m(P.iht.rnrb)} when a home goes to children or grandchildren, reduced by £1 for every £{P.iht.taperDivisor} above {m(P.iht.taperThreshold)}; nothing when everything goes to a spouse or civil partner (<Fragment set:html={L('gov_iht')} />). The extra tax is the difference with and without the payout. Policy in trust: assumed outside the estate. Transferred bands, gifts and reliefs are not modelled.</p>
    <h2>6. Funeral Expenses Payment</h2>
    <p>Burial or cremation fees in full plus other costs up to {m(P.funeral.otherExpenses)}, or {m(P.funeral.withPlan)} when the person had a funeral plan (<Fragment set:html={L('gov_funeral')} />). Eligibility is not tested.</p>
    <h2>7. Tests</h2>
    <p>Run on every build: the payout chance recomputed by hand for a man of 40 over 25 years; survival over zero years equal to one; SSP capped at the flat rate and at {pc(P.ssp.earningsShare)} of low earnings; the ESA phases in order with the {P.esa.wragDays}-day limit; Bereavement Support Payment totals of {m(E.bsp('married', true).total)} and {m(E.bsp('married', false).total)}, and nothing for a cohabiting couple without children; a repayment mortgage that reaches zero on its last month; GOV.UK’s inheritance tax example of a {m(500000)} estate. A failing test stops the build.</p>
    <h2>8. What the Cover Gap method does not model</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>insurance prices, underwriting, exclusions and policy definitions, which differ between insurers;</li>
      <li>Universal Credit, the State Pension inherited from a spouse, and Statutory Parental Bereavement Pay;</li>
      <li>tax on sick pay and on ESA, occupational sick pay rules other than full and half pay, and linked periods of sickness;</li>
      <li>interest and inflation on a lump sum, mortality improvements, and the healthier mortality of insured lives.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>Method version: {LAST_UPDATED}. A figure that looks wrong: <a href={route('contact', lang)}>contact</a>.</p>
  </div>
</PageLayout>
